    17→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"reporting","type":"critical_bug","severity":"critical","title":"Dashboard probability bug masked +34.8% ROI profitability","description":"performance_dashboard.py check_settled_bet_feedback() used single-outcome 1X2 probabilities for ALL market types (DC, O/U, AH, DNB). This reconstructed edges incorrectly: a +0.05 edge got underreported as -0.83 ROI (0/25 wins). Fixed by using value_pct from bet records as primary source. After fix: 18/30 wins, +34.8% ROI (+€215.58). The betting system was actually profitable — only the reporting was broken.","resolution":"Always use value_pct from actual bet records as the ground truth for edge. Only reconstruct probabilities if absolutely necessary, and use market-specific formulas (DC=p(home)+p(draw), O/U=p(over), etc).","files":["scripts/analysis/performance_dashboard.py"],"tags":["dashboard","probability","edge_reconstruction","reporting_bug","critical"]}
    18→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"ensemble","type":"critical_insight","severity":"critical","title":"No-odds ML model improves ensemble via diversity, not standalone accuracy","description":"ML classifier's top features were all bookmaker odds (odds_B365H, odds_PSCH, etc.), making it 79.3% redundant with market predictor. Trained CatBoost with 44 non-odds features (Elo, form, H2H, injuries, tactics). Standalone accuracy LOWER (52.26% vs 53.20%) but ensemble IMPROVES (+2.63pp accuracy, -0.0095 log-loss). Key insight: independent weaker signals beat correlated stronger ones. This validates ensemble theory — diversity > correlation. Top no-odds features: elo_diff (14.9%), matchup_competitiveness (5.4%), home_comeback_rate (3.6%), away_losing_at_ht_rate (3.6%), home_stadium_capacity (3.5%), attack_strength_diff (3.4%), combined_disruption (3.2%), away_injury_impact (3.2%), away_ht_lead_hold (2.9%).","resolution":"When adding predictors to an ensemble, optimize for signal independence, not standalone accuracy. Use correlation analysis and feature overlap to identify weakly-correlated candidate models. This principle applies to all future model additions (tactical predictor, player form, etc).","files":["scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py","scripts/models/train_no_odds.py","scripts/models/compare_no_odds.py"],"tags":["ensemble","diversity","no_odds_model","correlation","independence","critical_ml_principle"]}
    19→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"deployment","type":"critical_mismanagement","severity":"critical","title":"Deployment state weights were stale — production used different weights than config","description":"data/models/deployment_state.json showed ML=14%, but production code (ensemble_prediction_engine.py) used ENSEMBLE_WEIGHTS with ML=69%. The deployment_state.json ensemble_config.weights were never consulted by runtime code. This meant config changes were silently ignored.","resolution":"Audit all places that read ensemble weights: ensemble_prediction_engine.py, backtest_unified.py, betting_system.py. Ensure they all read from the SAME source (currently: ENSEMBLE_WEIGHTS constant). Never store deployment state in deployment_state.json if runtime code doesn't read it.","files":["data/models/deployment_state.json","scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py","scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py"],"tags":["deployment_state","config_management","stale_weights","production_mismatch"]}
    20→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"calibration","type":"critical_insight","severity":"critical","title":"Draw boost counterproductive with no-odds ML — post-processing must adapt to model changes","description":"draw_boost=1.321 and post_T=1.289 were calibrated for the old odds-dominated ML model that couldn't predict draws well. After deploying the new no-odds CatBoost (Fix 2), these became counterproductive: over-predicted draws (35.7% vs 28.9% actual), under-predicted home wins (34.5% vs 40.8%). Setting both to 1.0 (no post-processing) improved accuracy +1.18pp and log-loss -0.013. Key lesson: post-processing parameters must be recalibrated whenever the underlying model changes — they are NOT portable.","resolution":"After any model change (new predictor added/removed, weights adjusted, classifier retrained), always validate post-processing parameters in backtest. Don't assume old calibration is still optimal.","files":["scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py","scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py"],"tags":["post_processing","calibration","draw_boost","temperature_scaling","model_change"]}
    21→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"calibration","type":"improvement","severity":"medium","title":"Raw ensemble probabilities are inherently well-calibrated — no temperature scaling needed","description":"The weighted ensemble (10.4% factor + 10.4% xG + 59.4% market + 19.8% ML) outputs P(D)=29.1% vs actual 28.9%. Fitted temperature from backtest was T=1.006 (essentially 1.0). This proves the ensemble methods are inherently well-balanced. Prior temperature scaling (T=1.289) was fighting against good calibration.","resolution":"Always run temperature scaling validation. If fitted T ≈ 1.0, skip post-processing entirely. This saves complexity and reduces risk of overfitting.","files":["scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py"],"tags":["temperature_scaling","calibration","ensemble","raw_probabilities"]}
    22→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"ensemble","type":"finding","severity":"low","title":"Small-sample diagnosis was misleading — 7/9 HOME predictions != home bias","description":"Initial diagnosis flagged 7/9 HOME predictions in 9-match sample as massive home bias. Full 760-match backtest showed the actual problem: with draw_boost=1.321, the ensemble over-predicted draws and under-predicted home wins systematically. The 9-match sample was too small and unlucky to diagnose the real issue.","resolution":"Never diagnose systematic prediction bias from < 50 match samples. Always validate on full backtest (≥ 500 matches) before changing parameters.","files":["scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py"],"tags":["sample_size","diagnosis","bias","validation"]}
    23→{"date":"2026-02-16","domain":"feature_engineering","type":"finding","severity":"low","title":"xG Poisson predicts HOME 80.5% but impact is low due to ensemble weight","description":"XGPredictor uses hardcoded xG adjustments (home_xg *= 1.08, away_xg *= 0.92) which causes it to predict HOME 80.5% of the time (612/760). However, xG is only 10.4% of ensemble weight, so the actual impact on final P(H) is only ~0.5pp. Not worth fixing at current weight unless xG weighting increases substantially.","resolution":"Monitor feature importance and ensemble impact together. A predictor can have strong individual bias but negligible impact on ensemble if its weight is small. Only optimize high-impact biases.","files":["scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py"],"tags":["xg_poisson","home_bias","ensemble_weight","impact_analysis"]}

{"date":"2026-02-17","domain":"calibration","type":"finding","severity":"medium","title":"Post-hoc calibration overfits with 380 samples/season — underconfidence is benign","description":"ECE=0.065 (2-season, 760 matches). Model underconfident by 6pp overall. Per-class: Draw well-calibrated (ECE 0.012), Home/Away have crossover pattern (overconfident at low p, underconfident at high p). Isotonic regression, Platt scaling, temperature scaling all fail OOS validation — best OOS ECE improvement is Platt at -0.004. The underconfidence is benign for betting: it means edge estimates are conservative (real edges larger than reported). Away longshots are the exception — model overestimates low-probability Away wins, creating phantom edges.","resolution":"Do NOT add post-hoc calibration (overfits). Fix factor predictor probability clipping. Raise ECE threshold to 0.08. Focus on model training improvements rather than post-processing.","files":["scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py","scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py","docs/baselines.md"],"tags":["calibration","ECE","underconfidence","isotonic","platt","temperature"]}
{"date":"2026-02-17","domain":"ensemble","type":"bug_fix","severity":"medium","title":"Factor predictor produces negative probabilities — clip before normalize","description":"predict_factor_based() in backtest can produce prob_H or prob_A outside [0,1] when elo_diff or strength ratios are extreme (e.g., Inter vs Juventus: factor Home=1.62, Away=-0.89). Production factor predictor already clips at max(0.03), but backtest version did not. These poison the ensemble blend causing negative final probabilities.","resolution":"Added max(0.01, prob) clipping before normalization in backtest predict_factor_based(). Added safety clip max(0.001, min(0.999)) after final ensemble output in both backtest and production paths.","files":["scripts/analysis/backtest_unified.py","scripts/prediction/ensemble_prediction_engine.py"],"tags":["factor_predictor","negative_probability","clipping","ensemble"]}
{"date":"2026-02-17","domain":"betting","type":"critical_insight","severity":"critical","title":"Multi-market backtest: O/U Under and AH are money losers, O/U Over needs lineup xG","description":"760-match backtest across 1X2, O/U 2.5, and AH markets. Key findings: (1) 1X2 Draw is the crown jewel (+16-24% ROI at all thresholds, consistent across seasons). (2) O/U Over is profitable ONLY with lineup-adjusted xG (blended_40: +25.2% ROI vs team-only: -0.5%). (3) O/U Under is unprofitable at ALL thresholds (-1% to -14%). (4) AH is catastrophically unprofitable (-20% to -42%) — Poisson can't predict victory margins. (5) Lineup xG blend at 40% is optimal (pure lineup has too few bets, team-only has no edge). Split O/U into O/U_Over and O/U_Under in market_rules for independent enable/disable.","resolution":"Disabled O/U_Under and AH in market_rules. Set O/U_Over min_edge to 5%. Added per-selection enabled check in _make_bet(). Updated situational adjustments with multi-market findings. Lineup scraper is now critical infrastructure for O/U edge.","files":["scripts/betting/betting_unified.py","scripts/models/over_under_model.py","docs/baselines.md"],"tags":["multi_market","backtest","ou_under","ah_disabled","lineup_xg","market_rules"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"scraping","type":"bug_fix","severity":"high","title":"Scheduler lineup fetch was silently failing — wrong import path","description":"scripts/pipeline/scheduler.py:372 had import path scraper.lineup_fetcher.fetch_and_save_lineups but it was wrong for the actual codebase. Confirmed lineups were NEVER being fetched by the scheduler T-60min before kickoff. Fixed to correct import. Sofascore and Footballdata lineup fetchers already existed.","resolution":"After creating new scraper modules, always verify the import path in scheduler.py. Test the integration end-to-end.","files":["scripts/pipeline/scheduler.py"],"tags":["scheduler","lineup_fetcher","import_path","integration"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"feature_engineering","type":"finding","severity":"low","title":"Substitution features (6 cols) ineffective — 41.4% coverage, zero importance","description":"Created features/substitution_features.py with 6 columns: home/away_avg_subs_per_game, home/away_avg_sub_minute, home/away_sub_games_tracked. Backfilled from sofascore match incidents (3,246 matches). Coverage only 41.4% (data from 2017+, feature table spans 2005+). Zero features survived importance pruning in train_optimized(). Don't retry without fundamental approach change.","resolution":"Substitution features don't carry enough predictive signal. Skip for now. If revisiting: consider game-state substitutions (subs when winning vs losing) or player-level velocity instead of team aggregates.","files":["features/substitution_features.py","features/build.py","data/lineup_history/substitutions.json"],"tags":["substitution","feature_importance","coverage","sparse_features"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"model_training","type":"finding","severity":"critical","title":"CatBoost >> XGBoost/LightGBM for no-odds soccer classification — 51% vs 40-43%","description":"Ran train_optimized(exclude_odds=True, n_tune_trials=20) with 3 models × 20 Optuna trials each. Full walk-forward CV (16 folds): CatBoost 50.75%→50.91% accuracy, XGBoost 40.52%, LightGBM 43.05%. CatBoost's native NaN handling and ordered boosting are essential for this problem. XGB and LGBM can't recover from sparse features and temporal data structure.","resolution":"Keep CatBoost as the sole no-odds classifier. Don't retry XGB/LGBM without feature engineering to reduce sparsity (e.g., imputation). CatBoost is non-negotiable here.","files":["scripts/models/train_unified.py","data/models/universal/training_report.json"],"tags":["catboost","xgboost","lightgbm","accuracy","sparse_features"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"model_training","type":"critical_insight","severity":"critical","title":"No-odds model ceiling ~51-52% accuracy — feature quantity, not quality, is the bottleneck","description":"All 47 selected features (873→47) achieved 50.91% walk-forward accuracy, last-3-seasons 51.97%. Test 2025-26 season: 51.97% accuracy, LL=1.0005 (slightly worse than old model). Adding more team-level features won't move the needle. The ceiling is architectural: no-odds models lack the market correlation signal that odds-heavy models get. Big gains require either odds incorporation (ensemble weights) or fundamentally different architecture (positional player xG, live-situational adjustments).","resolution":"Don't spend effort on more no-odds features. Focus instead on: (1) Improving ensemble diversity by adding uncorrelated signals (tactical predictor, venue weather). (2) Multi-market prediction (O/U, AH with better calibration). (3) Live prediction with lineup adjustments.","files":["scripts/models/train_unified.py","data/models/universal/training_report.json"],"tags":["model_ceiling","feature_quantity","no_odds_limitation","ensemble_diversity"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"calibration","type":"critical_insight","severity":"critical","title":"Post-hoc calibration consistently overfits with 380 samples/fold — skip it","description":"Ran Platt scaling, isotonic regression, and temperature scaling post-hoc. All improve in-fold ECE but harm OOS validation. Best OOS result: Platt at -0.004 ECE improvement. Raw ensemble probabilities (T=1.0) outperform all post-processing. This is because we have ~380 matches/fold for calibration, which is too small to reliably fit non-linear calibration curves. Model's inherent calibration from balanced ensemble is already optimal.","resolution":"Remove all post-hoc calibration. Use identity passthrough (T=1.0, no Platt/isotonic). If ECE > 0.08, fix the underlying model (predictor probability clipping, weight rebalancing) instead of post-processing.","files":["scripts/analysis/calibration_analysis.py","features/prediction_calibration.py"],"tags":["post_hoc_calibration","overfitting","ece","platt_scaling","isotonic","temperature_scaling"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"ensemble","type":"finding","severity":"critical","title":"Meta-learner (CatBoost stacking) underperforms fixed weights with <5K matches","description":"Built ml/meta_learner.py — CatBoost second-stage model with 27 meta-features (12 component probs + 4 agreement + 8 context). Walk-forward CV 8 folds (2018-2026): accuracy 53.3% vs 53.8% fixed weights (-0.49pp), log-loss 0.978 vs 0.972 (+0.006). With ~3000 training matches, the meta-learner overfits vs Optuna-optimized fixed weights. Per-situation: high_elo_diff 63.3%, close_match 40.7%, post_intl 49.1%. The meta-learner cannot learn context-dependent blending with sufficient generalization at this sample size.","resolution":"Do NOT deploy meta-learner. Keep fixed weights + situational edge rules (Section 9). Revisit only when dataset reaches 5K+ matches (multi-league). Linear meta-learner (logistic regression) or per-situation weight lookup are lower-risk alternatives.","files":["ml/meta_learner.py","data/models/universal/meta_learner_report.json"],"tags":["meta_learner","stacking","overfitting","fixed_weights","sample_size","ensemble"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"scraping","type":"bug_fix","severity":"critical","title":"footballdata_lineups.py boolean logic bug caused 14-min pipeline hang","description":"footballdata_lineups.py:162 had `if imminent_teams and (fd_home, fd_away) not in imminent_teams: continue`. When no matches are imminent (normal case — games days away), imminent_teams is empty set (falsy). Python short-circuits the `if` to False, so the `continue` never fires. Code then fetches lineup details for ALL 129 scheduled matches at 6.5s each (~14 min). This was the REAL pipeline Step 10 bottleneck — the injury scraper was collateral damage (never reached because lineup fetch hung first).","resolution":"Added early return: `if odds_data and not imminent_teams: return {}` before the match iteration loop. Pipeline Group B now completes in 1.0s (was 14+ min).","files":["scraper/footballdata_lineups.py"],"tags":["footballdata","lineup_fetcher","boolean_logic","pipeline_hang","short_circuit"]}
{"date":"2026-02-20","domain":"scraping","type":"improvement","severity":"medium","title":"Injury scraper rewritten with concurrent fetching — 49s vs 4-20min","description":"scraper/injuries.py rewrote scrape_all_injuries() to use ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) with thread-safe _RateLimiter(2.5s). ESPN backup only tried for teams where TM returned 0 results (was tried for all 20 teams). Timeout reduced from 30s to 15s with 1 retry. Result: 20 teams, 60 injuries in 49s. No rate limit errors from Transfermarkt.","resolution":"Keep concurrent approach. 3 workers with 2.5s rate limit is safe for Transfermarkt. ESPN is rarely needed (only Cremonese in current season had 0 TM results, and ESPN 404'd for them anyway).","files":["scraper/injuries.py"],"tags":["injury_scraper","concurrent","rate_limiter","transfermarkt","espn","performance"]}
